## Migration: `Thread.Abort` Timeout → Cooperative Cancellation (.NET 8)

### Pattern Identified: **Timeout Enforcement**

Your code forcibly aborts a thread after a timeout. In .NET 6+, `Thread.Abort()` throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` (`SYSLIB0006`). The replacement is `CancellationTokenSource.CancelAfter` with task-based execution.

### Migrated Code

```csharp
public class TimedExecutor
{
    public T ExecuteWithTimeout<T>(Func<T> work, TimeSpan timeout)
    {
        using var cts = new CancellationTokenSource(timeout);
        var task = Task.Run(work, cts.Token);

        try
        {
            // Task.WaitAsync throws OperationCanceledException on timeout
            return task.WaitAsync(cts.Token).GetAwaiter().GetResult();
        }
        catch (OperationCanceledException) when (cts.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            throw new TimeoutException(
                $"Operation exceeded {timeout.TotalSeconds}s timeout");
        }
    }

    // Preferred: async overload
    public async Task<T> ExecuteWithTimeoutAsync<T>(
        Func<T> work, TimeSpan timeout, CancellationToken externalToken = default)
    {
        using var cts = CancellationTokenSource.CreateLinkedTokenSource(externalToken);
        cts.CancelAfter(timeout);

        try
        {
            return await Task.Run(work, cts.Token).WaitAsync(cts.Token);
        }
        catch (OperationCanceledException) when (externalToken.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            throw; // External cancellation — propagate as-is
        }
        catch (OperationCanceledException) when (cts.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            throw new TimeoutException(
                $"Operation exceeded {timeout.TotalSeconds}s timeout");
        }
    }
}
```

### If the work itself must cooperate with cancellation:

```csharp
// Pass the token INTO the work so it can check for cancellation
public T ExecuteWithTimeout<T>(Func<CancellationToken, T> work, TimeSpan timeout)
{
    using var cts = new CancellationTokenSource(timeout);
    try
    {
        return Task.Run(() => work(cts.Token), cts.Token)
                   .WaitAsync(cts.Token)
                   .GetAwaiter().GetResult();
    }
    catch (OperationCanceledException) when (cts.IsCancellationRequested)
    {
        throw new TimeoutException(
            $"Operation exceeded {timeout.TotalSeconds}s timeout");
    }
}
```

### Key Pitfalls

| Pitfall | Explanation |
|---------|-------------|
| **Cancellation ≠ abortion** | If `work()` doesn't check the token, it keeps running in the background even after timeout. The caller gets the `TimeoutException` but the work isn't killed. |
| **Not disposing `CancellationTokenSource`** | Leaks timers. Always wrap in `using`. |
| **Swallowing `OperationCanceledException`** | Only catch it at the orchestration boundary to convert to `TimeoutException`. |
| **`Task.Run` with captured token** | Passing `cts.Token` to `Task.Run` only prevents scheduling if already cancelled — it does NOT terminate running work. |
| **Blocking call inside `work`** | `Thread.Sleep`, synchronous I/O, etc. won't respond to cancellation. Replace with `await Task.Delay(ms, token)` or async I/O. |

### What was removed and why

| Old API | Why it fails in .NET 8 |
|---------|----------------------|
| `Thread.Abort()` | Throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` |
| `catch (ThreadAbortException)` | Never thrown — dead code |
| `Thread.ResetAbort()` | Throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` |

### Bottom line

The fundamental shift: **you cannot forcibly stop code that doesn't cooperate**. If `work` is a long-running computation you control, thread `token.ThrowIfCancellationRequested()` calls through it. If it's uncooperative third-party code, the only true kill mechanism is `Process.Kill` on a child process.
